#lg A	kirugu


@(A.1 = 1-8) He has abandoned his cow-pen and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. The wild bull has abandoned his cow-pen and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. The lord of all the lands has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. @wDN{Enlil} has abandoned the shrine @wSN{Nibru} and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. His wife @wDN{Ninlil} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Ninlil} has abandoned that house, the @wTN{Ki-ur}, and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. The queen of @wSN{Keš} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Ninmaḫ} has abandoned that house @wSN{Keš} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold.

@(A.9 = 9-18) She of @wSN{Isin} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Ninisina} has abandoned the shrine @wTN{Egal-maḫ} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. The queen of @wSN{Unug} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Inana} has abandoned that house @wSN{Unug} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Nanna} has abandoned @wSN{Urim} and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. @wDN{Suen} has abandoned @wTN{E-kiš-nu-ŋal} and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. His wife @wDN{Ningal} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Ningal} has abandoned her @wTN{Agrun-kug} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. The wild bull of @wSN{Eridug} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. @wDN{Enki} has abandoned that house @wSN{Eridug} and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold.

@(A.18A = 18A-26)  @vart[t222.v1,t222.v1]{@varn{1 ms. adds 1 line:} …… @noteref{partially preserved name of a goddess} has abandoned that house @wSN{Larag} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. } @wDN{Šara} has abandoned @wTN{E-maḫ} and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. @wDN{Ud-saḫara} has abandoned that house @wSN{Umma} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Bau} has abandoned @wSN{Iri-kug} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. She has abandoned her flooded chamber and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. Her son @wDN{Ab-Bau} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. @wDN{Ab-Bau} has abandoned @wTN{Ma-gu-ena} and has let the breezes haunt his sheepfold. The protective goddess of the holy house has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold.

@(A.26 = 26-34) The protective goddess has abandoned @wTN{E-tar-sirsir} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. The mother of @wSN{Lagaš} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Ŋatumdug} has abandoned that house @wSN{Lagaš} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. She of @wSN{Niŋin} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. The great queen has abandoned that house @wSN{Sirara} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. She of @wSN{Kinirša} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Dumuzid-abzu} has abandoned that house @wSN{Kinirša} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. She of @wSN{Gu-aba} has abandoned it and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold. @wDN{Ninmarki} has abandoned the shrine @wSN{Gu-aba} and has let the breezes haunt her sheepfold.

#trailer rubric	kirugu


@(A.35 = 35) 1st @fsux{kirugu}.

#lg B	jicgijal


@(B.36 = 36-37) She has let the breeze haunt her sheepfold, she groans grievously over it. O cow, your lowing no longer fills the byre, the cow-pen no longer brings joy (?) to the prince.

#trailer rubric	jicgijal


@(B.38 = 38) Its @fsux{ŋišgiŋal}.

#lg C	kirugu


@(C.39 = 39-46) O city, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. Your lament is bitter, O city, the lament made for you. In his righteous destroyed city its lament is bitter. In his righteous destroyed @wSN{Urim}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. Your lament is bitter, O city, the lament made for you. In his destroyed @wSN{Urim} its lament is bitter. How long will your bitter lament grieve your lord who weeps? How long will your bitter lament grieve @wDN{Nanna} who weeps?

@(C.47 = 47-55) O brick-built @wSN{Urim}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O @wTN{E-kiš-nu-ŋal}, your lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O shrine @wTN{Agrun-kug}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O great place @wTN{Ki-ur}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O shrine @wSN{Nibru}, city, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O brick-built @wTN{E-kur}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O @wTN{Ŋa-ŋiš-šua}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O @wTN{Ubšu-unkena}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O brick-built @wSN{Iri-kug}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you.

@(C.56 = 56-63) O @wTN{E-tar-sirsir}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O @wTN{Ma-gu-ena}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O brick-built @wSN{Isin}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O shrine @wTN{Egal-maḫ}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O brick-built @wSN{Unug}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. O brick-built @wDN{Eridug}, the lament is bitter, the lament made for you. How long will your bitter lament grieve your lord who weeps? How long will your bitter lament grieve @wDN{Nanna} who weeps?

@(C.64 = 64-71) O city, your name exists but you have been destroyed. O city, your wall rises high but your Land has perished. O my city, like an innocent ewe your lamb has been torn from you. O @wSN{Urim}, like an innocent goat your kid has perished. O city, your rites have been alienated from you, your powers have been changed into alien powers. How long will your bitter lament grieve your lord who weeps? How long will your bitter lament grieve @wDN{Nanna} who weeps?

#trailer rubric	kirugu


@(C.72 = 72) 2nd @fsux{kirugu}.

#lg D	jicgijal


@(D.73 = 73-74) In his righteous destroyed city its lament is bitter. In his destroyed @wSN{Urim} its lament is bitter.

#trailer rubric	jicgijal


@(D.75 = 75) Its @fsux{ŋišgiŋal}.

#lg E	kirugu


@(E.76 = 76-84) Together with the lord whose house had been devastated, his city was given over to tears. Together with @wDN{Nanna} whose Land had perished, @wSN{Urim} joined the lament. The good woman, to disquiet the lord concerning his city, @wDN{Ningal}, to give him no rest concerning his Land, approached him for the sake of his city -- bitterly she weeps. She approached the lord for the sake of his house -- bitterly she weeps. She approached him for the sake of his devastated city -- bitterly she weeps. She approached him for the sake of his devastated house -- before him she makes its bitter lament.

@(E.85 = 85-94) The woman, after she had composed her song (?) for the tearful @fsux{balaŋ} instrument, herself utters softly a lamentation for the silent house: @q[Ningal,]{“The storm that came to be -- its lamentation hangs heavy on me. Raging about because of the storm, I am the woman for whom the storm came to be. The storm that came to be -- its lamentation hangs heavy on me. The bitter storm having come to be for me during the day, I trembled on account of that day but I did not flee before the day's violence. Because of this debilitating storm I could not see a good day for my rule, not one good day for my rule.”}

@(E.95 = 95-100A) @q{“The bitter lament having come to be for me during the night, I trembled on account of that night but I did not flee before the night's violence. The awesomeness of this storm, destructive as the flood, truly hangs heavy on me. Because of its existence, in my nightly sleeping place, even in my nightly sleeping place truly there was no peace for me. Nor, because of this debilitating storm, was the quiet of my sleeping place, not even the quiet of my sleeping place, allowed to me.  @vart[t222.v2,t222.v2]{@varn{2 mss. add 1 line:} Truly I did not forsake my Land. }”}

@(E.101 = 101-111) @q{“Because there was bitterness in my Land, I trudged the earth like a cow for its calf. My Land was not granted succcess. Because there was bitter distress in my city, I beat my wings like a bird of heaven and flew to my city; and my city was destroyed in its foundations; and @wSN{Urim} perished where it lay. Because the hand of the storm appeared above, I screamed and cried to it @q[Ningal,]{“Return, O storm, to the plain”}. The storm's breast did not rise.”}

@(E.112 = 112-122) @q{“To me, the woman, in the @wTN{Agrun-kug}, my house of queenship, they did not grant a reign of distant days. Indeed they established weeping and lamentation for me. As for the house which used to be where the spirit of the black-headed people was soothed, instead of its festivals wrath and terror indeed multiply. Because of this debilitating storm, depression, and lament and bitterness, lament and bitterness have been brought into my house, the favourable place, my devastated righteous house upon which no eye had been cast. My house founded by the righteous was pushed over on its side like a garden fence.”}

@(E.123 = 123-132) @q{“For @wTN{E-kiš-nu-ŋal}, my house of royalty, the good house, my house which has been given over to tears, they granted to me as its lot and share: its building, falsely, and its perishing, truly. Wind and rain have been made to fall on it, as onto a tent, a shelter on the denuded harvest ground, as onto a shelter on the denuded harvest ground. @wSN{Urim}, my all-surpassing chamber, the house and the smitten city, all have been uprooted. Like a shepherd's sheepfold it has been uprooted. The swamp has swallowed my possessions accumulated in the city.”}

